==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ang|haldan||Anglian}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*haldan}}, from {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*haldaną||to watch, look after}}.

Cognate with {{cog|ofs|halda}} ({{cog|fy|hâlde}}), {{cog|osx|haldan}} ({{cog|nds|holen}}), {{cog|odt|haldan}} ({{cog|nl|houden}}), {{cog|goh|haltan}} ({{cog|de|halten}}), {{cog|non|halda}} ({{cog|sv|hålla}}, {{cog|da|holde}}), {{cog|got|𐌷𐌰𐌻𐌳𐌰𐌽}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|healdan}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb}} {{tlb|ang|West Saxon}}

# to keep watch over (cattle etc.)
#*  ''Old English Heptateuch'', [https://archive.org/details/oldenglishheptat0000unse/page/14/mode/2up?view=theater Genesis 4:9]
#*: {{quote|ang|''...sċeolde iċ mīnne broþor '''healdon'''?''|...should I watch over my brother?}}
# to [[hold]] fast, to [[grasp]]
# to [[contain]]
# to [[possess]]
#* '''c. 992''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Homilies_of_the_Anglo-Saxon_Church/II "Sermon on the Nativiity of Our Lord"]
#*: {{quote|ang|Ðyllīce word María '''hēold''' ārǣfniġende on hire heortan.|Such words Mary '''held''', pondering them in her heart.}}
# to [[keep]], hold, [[preserve]] something in a specific position or state
#* {{quote-book|ang|text=''Iċ… hlūde ċirme, '''healde''' mīne wīsan, hlēoþre ne mīþe,…''|t=I… loudly cry out, '''hold''' my tone, don't hide a sound,…|title=Exeter Book Riddle 8|year=10th century|url=https://www.oldenglishaerobics.net/riddles.html#w0000085}}
# to [[keep to]], [[maintain]], [[observe]] a custom or habit
#* '''c. 994''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], ''[https://books.google.co.id/books?id=iqCU4ZV4pjoC&pg=PA19 On the Seasons of the Year]''
#*: {{quote|ang|Rōmanisc̄e lēodan ongynnað heora ġēar æfter hǣðenum ġewunan on winterlīċere tīde. Ebrei '''healdað''' heora geares annginn on lenctenlīcre emnihte. Đā Grēcisċan onginnað hyra ġēar æt ðām sunnstede; ⁊ ða Egiptisċan on hærfest.|The Roman people begin theirs, after the heathen custom, in the winter time. The Hebrews '''observe''' the beginning of their year on the vernal equinox. The Greeks begin their year at the solstice; and the Egyptians in the fall.}}
# {{lb|ang|intransitive}} to maintain one’s position against an enemy

